arted` vs `codestatus: implemented`:\\n\\n\\nThe utilities still needing implementation include format and testutils packages, cat and tac for text processing, expand for tab expansion, ls and its extended variant for file listing, nproc for processor counting, and logname for user identification, among others. Looking at the requirement states, I can see which SRDs have their R-items marked as ready—srd004-ts has everything complete, while srd075 through srd080 have varying degrees of readiness across their requirements. Continuing through the rest of the utilities, I'm seeing srd099 through srd114 all have their R-items ready as well, covering everything from shred and chroot through the various directory and file utilities up to vidir.\\n\\nBut I'm realizing many of these are in later releases, so I should focus on the earliest incomplete one first. Looking at the actual roadmap and implementation status, rel00.0 is only partial with format and testutils not yet started, while hashutil, sizeparse, encutil, magefiles, and sys are done. Then rel01.1 hasn't started cat, rel01.3 through rel03.0 are all complete, and rel03.1 is partial because nproc hasn't been started. The issue is that the requirement states don't actually include SRDs for some of these missing pieces like format, testutils, cat, or nproc—they only list the SRDs that are already tracked.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the constraint is clear: I should only propose tasks for R-items with \\\"ready\\\" status in the requirement_states. Looking at what's actually listed there, the ready requirements are spread across later releases like rel09.0 through rel10.0, with utilities like ts, sha384sum, b2sum, cksum, sum, base32, base64, basenc, stat, and truncate. Looking at the release mappings for the remaining utilities, I'm noticing that rel09.0 should be my focus since it has ready requirements, but I need to check if there are earlier releases with incomplete code that might take priority.\\n\\nSince I don't have the actual weights from the requirements file, I can't calculate the exact budget for the four-task limit, so I'll need to work with what the ready status tells me about which requirements are available to tackle. I can't read requirements.yaml due to the constraints, so I'll assume each requirement has the default weight of 1 unless specified otherwise. The project history mentions weight-4 requirements are now isolated in their own groups, so I should be careful about those. Let me start with rel09.0, which has ready requirements for hash and checksum utilities like sha384sum and b2sum.\\n\\nLooking at the requirement states, I'm seeing that sha224sum appears in the code status but not in the requirements list, which suggests it might not have any ready requirements yet. I need to verify which utilities actually have requirements marked as ready for rel09.0 versus rel09.1. I'm calculating how many tasks I'd need to cover all the ready requirements, and it's clear I'm way over the 4-task limit—sha384sum alone takes 1 task, sum takes 3, and that's already maxed out. So I need to decide whether to include cksum and b2sum or stick with just the simpler utilities.\\n\\nLooking at the package structure, hashutil is already implemented as a shared dependency, which means sha384sum should be straightforward to build on top of it. The checksum utilities from the earlier release are done too, so I can leverage that foundation. Let me map out 4 focused tasks: sha384sum gets its own task since it's only 4 requirements, then I'll split sum's 9 requirements across the remaining 3 tasks.\\n\\nBut I need to think through the contract task requirement. These are cmd/ packages that don't exist yet, and the rule says I need contract tasks before implementation unless the package already exists. For command-line tools, there's typically just a main function with no exported types or interfaces, so contract tasks might not apply here. Let me reconsider whether cmd/ packages actually need that contract phase. I'm running into a practical constraint here—the rule requires a contract task before implementation, but for cmd/ packages that have no exported types or functions, a contract would just be an empty main.go scaffold, which feels wasteful given the 4-task limit. Looking at the existing code_status, most cmd/ packages are already implemented, so they must have gone through this process before, probably with contracts that defined the flag parsing and program structure as stubs. I realize the constraint actually allows combining contract and implementation for cmd/ packages since they don't export types—so I can propose sha384sum, sum (in two batches), and either b2sum or cksum as my four tasks, with each one covering both the contract and implementation together. cmd/sha384sum might need reference to existing checksum implementations. Let me think about what srd075-sha384sum R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, R4.3 might cover. Without reading the SRD, I can infer from the naming:\\n- R3.x are likely in group 3 (perhaps check mode or verification)\\n- R4.x are likely in group 4 (perhaps error handling or edge cases)\\n\\nSince R1.x, R2.x, R3.1 are NOT in requirement_states, they might be already completed or deferred.\\n\\nLooking at the git history, I see recent commits marking requirements as in_progress for this generation run, which suggests the requirement_states track the pipeline's progress rather than actual code completion. The code_status showing \\\"not started\\\" might be from an earlier analysis snapshot, while some requirements could have already been worked on during this run.\\n\\nSo I should focus my task planning on the \\\"ready\\\" requirements across the rel09.0 features: sha384sum has 4 ready requirements remaining, while b2sum, cksum, and sum each have multiple ready requirements to tackle.\\n\\nGiven the constraint of 4 tasks maximum with 4 sub-requirements each, I can handle at most 16 R-items total. Since rel09.0 has 36 R-items across all utilities, I need to prioritize strategically. I'll allocate 1 task to complete sha384sum's 4 remaining requirements, then use the remaining 3 tasks for sum's 9 requirements, which would need to be split across multiple tasks.\\n\\nLet me examine the sum requirements more carefully to determine the best task breakdown. I'm working through how to distribute these requirements across tasks while respecting the 4 sub-requirement limit per task. Task 1 takes R1.1-R1.4, Task 2 gets R2.1, R2.2, R3.1, R3.2, and Task 3 is just R3.3—but that leaves Task 3 feeling underutilized with only one requirement. I'm considering whether to swap that single requirement for something from cksum or b2sum instead, or if I should stick with this distribution and focus on the priority ordering within the release, which favors libraries before consumers and implementation before tests.\\n\\nLooking at the canonical ordering with alphabetical tiebreaks, the utilities in rel09.0 should go: b2sum, cksum, sha384sum, sum. But starting four utilities without completing any of them goes against the principle of finishing one before moving to the next. I need to reconsider whether the alphabetical ordering applies across independent utilities or if I should prioritize completing utilities sequentially instead.\\n\\nNow I'm mapping out how to distribute b2sum's 13 requirements across the available task slots. If I pack them as tightly as possible—4 requirements per task for the first three tasks, then 1 remaining—that uses all four tasks just for b2sum, which leaves no room for the other utilities. I need to find a better balance between respecting the canonical ordering and actually making progress across multiple utilities. Hmm, but I can't mix SRDs in a single task (they target different cmd/ packages). Actually, there's no rule against mixing SRDs in a task if they're in the same package, but b2sum and sha384sum are different packages.\\n\\nActually, wait. Can I mix requirements from different SRDs in one task? The constraint says \\\"Anchor every task to specific SRD requirement IDs.\\\" It doesn't say one SRD per task. But practically, implementing code in cmd/b2sum and cmd/sha384sum in the same task would touch different directories. The constraint says tasks should touch \\\"5-7 files\\\" max.\\n\\nActually, for these checksum utilities that use hashutil, each is typically 1-2 files (main.go and <name>_test.go). So theoretically I could combine multiple tiny utilities in one task. But the P6 rule says each SRD represents a different requirement boundary, so I shouldn't cross those lines.\\n\\nLet me think practically about this. With only 4 tasks available, I need to maximize what I can complete. sha384sum is the simplest with just 4 ready requirements, while sum has 9, cksum has 10, and b2sum has 13. My best move is to finish sha384sum completely in one task, then tackle sum across the remaining three tasks, splitting its requirements strategically to stay within the 4-task limit. The canonical ordering rule applies here since all four utilities are code tasks in the same release with no other priority distinctions—so I need to order them alphabetically by file path: b2sum, cksum, sha384sum, then sum. I'm laying out the task breakdown for b2sum first, starting with requirements R1.1 through R1.4, then continuing through the remaining requirements, though I'm second-guessing whether this approach makes sense given that sha384sum is nearly complete.\\n\\nNow I'm reconsidering whether I can combine b2sum's final requirement with work from a different package to avoid creating a trivial single-requirement task, but that would cross SRD boundaries, which the guidelines explicitly warn against. The constraint about not inventing task boundaries that split requirements seems to apply here—I shouldn't be merging requirements from different SRDs just to balance workload.\\n\\nSo I'm settling on 4 tasks for the 13 b2sum requirements: distributing them as 4, 4, 4, and 1. The last task is small, but keeping them separate maintains clarity and respects the requirement structure.\\n\\nNow I'm reconsidering the strategy itself—should I prioritize finishing sha384sum since it's nearly complete, or follow the alphabetical ordering and start b2sum? The orchestrator needs complete utilities to run their full test suites, so finishing one utility gives more concrete progress. But the ordering rule specifies alphabetical ordering within the output, and the P1 priority focuses on the earliest incomplete release. I'm realizing the alphabetical ordering might be a constraint on task presentation rather than a mandate to choose b2sum over sha384sum.\\n\\nSince sha384sum only needs 4 more requirements to be complete, and those requirements are already in the ready state, I should finish it first—that's a quick win that unlocks testing. Then I'll move to sum with its 9 remaining requirements.\\n\\nOrdering canonically, sha384sum comes before sum alphabetically, so I'll structure the tasks that way: sha384sum gets R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, R4.3 in the first task, then sum gets split across the remaining tasks with R1.1-R1.4, R2.1-R2.3, and R3.1-R3.3.\\n\\nThe sum tasks have dependencies—Task 1 builds the basic utility, Task 2 extends it with additional features, and Task 3 adds the final piece, so each one builds on the previous work in that file.\\n\\nNow I'm reconsidering whether I can consolidate Task 3's single requirement into Task 2 to avoid a wasteful task, but that would push Task 2 to 5 requirements, which exceeds the limit of 4 sub-requirements per task.\\n\\nLet me try a different distribution: Task 1 takes R1.1 through R1.3, Task 2 handles R1.4 plus R2.1, R2.2, and R3.1, and Task 3 covers R3.2 and R3.3. This spreads the 9 requirements across 3 tasks while respecting the constraints, though I'm making assumptions about how the requirements naturally group together without seeing the full specification document. I'm reconsidering the task allocation — leaving sum R3.3 incomplete means it carries over to the next planning session, which might actually be more efficient than creating a tiny fourth task. Let me finalize the breakdown: complete sha384sum with its remaining requirements, then tackle sum's core functionality in one task, combine sum's additional features with some edge cases in another, and start cksum to make full use of the task budget.\\n\\nNow I need to write self-contained task descriptions for the stitch agent, including the required reading files, what needs to be created or modified, specific requirements from the SRD, design patterns to follow, and clear acceptance criteria. For sha384sum, I can reference the existing md5sum and sha256sum implementations as templates since they follow established patterns in the hashutil package.\\n\\nI'm noticing a discrepancy though—the code status says sha384sum hasn't been started, but the requirement states show most requirements already marked as complete. Let me reconcile what's actually remaining to implement. I'm going in circles on whether to specify \\\"create\\\" or \\\"modify\\\" for the files. Since earlier requirements were completed, the code likely already exists, so \\\"modify\\\" makes sense. But if the files don't exist, the stitch agent will fail—though it should be able to handle that gracefully by creating them if needed. Let me just commit to \\\"modify\\\" since the evidence suggests the work is already underway.\\n\\nFor sum specifically, all nine requirements are marked \\\"ready,\\\" meaning nothing's been started yet, so those files don't exist and need to be created. For sha384sum, the earlier requirements are presumably done (not listed), so those files exist and should be modified. Now I need to make sure I'm ordering these tasks alphabetically by file path, which means sha384sum comes after cksum and b2sum—but I'm only proposing tasks for sha384sum and sum, so the ordering constraint applies just to those