te the YAML, I need to make sure each task description is completely self-contained for the stitch agent—including what the utility does, the SRD path, specific R-items to implement, files to create or modify, existing code patterns to reference, build and test instructions, and any design constraints like SIGPIPE handling or differential testing requirements. I'm realizing I should be specific about what each SRD requirement entails based on typical checksum utility patterns, then point the stitch agent to the SRD for the full details and to existing code like cmd/sha256sum as reference implementations. Looking at the requirement states more carefully, I can see that R3.2, R4.1, R4.2, and R4.3 are marked as ready, while all the R1 and R2 requirements plus R3.1 are already complete. This means the check mode infrastructure is mostly done—the basic --check functionality is finished, and what's left is the output options like --warn, --quiet, --status, --strict, and --ignore-missing, along with the help, version, exit codes, and error handling pieces.\\n\\nFor sha384sum, I need to modify the existing code to add those remaining features and update the tests accordingly. For the sum utility, which is brand new, I'll need to create the main implementation and test file from scratch, following the project's patterns around SIGPIPE handling and differential testing.\\n\\nNow I'm reconsidering the task split—the constraint says to separate implementation from tests, but looking at how cmd/ packages work in this project, the test files are tightly coupled with the implementation using the differential testing pattern. With a 4-task limit, I can't afford separate test tasks, so I should combine implementation and tests for each utility. I'm realizing the test task for sha384sum depends on the implementation being complete first, since the existing test file needs new test cases added for the R3.2 and R4.1-R4.3 features. So I should order these as implementation first, then tests, which means I can fit sha384sum fully into two tasks and still have room for the sum implementation and tests. Writing YAML task definitions... I need to implement the remaining requirements for the sha384sum utility: adding control flags for check mode output, implementing help and version flags, and ensuring proper exit codes for failures. I'm realizing I shouldn't guess at what the specific SRD requirements actually contain—the stitch agent will read the SRD itself, so I should just reference the requirement IDs directly rather than inventing their details. The most useful thing I can provide is pointing to which SRD document to consult, which specific requirement IDs to implement, and what files need to be created or modified, then let the agent handle the specifics from there. I'm realizing I should be pragmatic here—the stitch agent will read the full SRD and fill in details, so I just need to anchor my task requirements to specific SRD IDs and describe what needs to be done at a high level. Since I can't read the files directly, I'll work with reasonable inferences from the checksum utility domain and the hashutil contract. I'm also noting that my 4 requirements per task align with the hard constraint from the requirements section, even though it's below the 5-8 guidance in P9. Now I'm structuring the test tasks, starting with sha384sum tests and mapping them to the relevant SRD requirements. Adding test requirements and acceptance criteria...\\n\\nNow moving on to the sum utility, which uses BSD or System V checksums rather than the SHA/MD5 approach. I'm reviewing the srd078 requirements covering core functionality, mode selection, and error handling, with the BSD algorithm being the default option.\\n\\nWriting sum implementation... utility implementing BSD and SysV checksum modes with requirements covering the BSD algorithm, stdin handling, multiple file processing, and output formatting. I'm sketching out design decisions like installing a SIGPIPE h